  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nSynoptically, for Tuesday through next Sunday, our upper level flow \nremains mainly from the northwest for the majority of the period. \nStarting around Wednesday, our flow will begin to transition to \nmainly zonal as a weak upper level ridge passes through. By \nSaturday, as the ridge makes its way to the Eastern CONUS, our flow \nwill transition to come from the southwest through Sunday. Friday \nthrough Sunday, Guidance indicates potential shortwaves moving \nthrough the County Warning Area (CWA). Sunday is where Guidance \nbegins to diverge on the upper level pattern. The majority of the \nlong range models show an upper level trough beginning to move into \nthe region, but guidance presents multiple solutions regarding the \nstrength and trajectory. With this being roughly seven days out, it \nwill be something to monitor as we move through the week. \n\nOverall, high temperatures across the Tri-State area should remain \nin the 70s and 80s. Tuesday is currently forecast to be the warmest \nday of the period, with highs in the low to mid 80s. Guidance, \nincluding the Canadian-NH and GFS, show a boundary or front moving \nthrough the region from the northwest Tuesday into Wednesday. As \nthis boundary moves through, temperatures should \"cool\" down to the \nupper 70s and low 80s before Saturdays highs briefly warm back to \nthe low to mid 80s. Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) are forecast \nto remain under 5% throughout the period. However, depending on the \nstrength of any shortwaves that pass through, PoPs could potentially \nincrease. \n\nAt this time, there is a low potential for fire weather concerns \nduring this period, Tuesday will be the primary day to monitor. \nRelative humidity (RH) values are forecast to drop around 20-25% for \nareas west of KS Hwy 27, with winds of 5-15 mph and gusts up to 25-\n30 mph. On Tuesday morning, winds should start out from the \nsouthwest before shifting clockwise to the east by the afternoon. \nWhile this does not reach critical fire weather, it is something to \nkeep an eye on especially with the potential boundary/front moving \nthrough which could lead to lower RH values. For the rest of the \nperiod, RH values mostly stay above 25%, with sustained winds of 5-\n15 mph and gusts in the 15-15 mph range. Friday through Sunday, \nbreezier afternoons with gusts reaching 30-40 mph are possible.",
